{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 11633 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "85a3f559199c4a01a442b4f69631965f59a4ae242f5410e9e8599c33fbc52312" :edb/db-before-t 11632 :edb/db-after-t 11633 :edb/tx-data [[13194139544945 50 #inst "2026-10-30T05:26:36.831Z" 13194139544945 17592186576411 true] [17592186059310 1078 #edb/ref 17592186059314 13194139544945 17592186576411 true] [17592186059370 1078 #edb/ref 17592186059375 13194139544945 17592186576411 true] [17592186059376 1078 #edb/ref 17592186059377 13194139544945 17592186576411 true] [17592186060448 1074 "Let's follow the standard five-step procedure.\n\n**Step 1:** Find the determinant of $A$.\n\n$$\n\\det(A)=(7)(-2)+(2)(3)+(1)(9)=1.\n$$\n\n**Step 2:** Form the matrix of minors. Delete row $i$ and column $j$ to obtain $M_{ij}$. For example,\n\n$$\nM_{11}=\\begin{vmatrix}3 & -1 \\\\ 4 & -2\\end{vmatrix}=-2.\n$$\n\nComputing the remaining minors gives\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -3 & 9 \\\\ -8 & -11 & 34 \\\\ -5 & -7 & 21\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n**Step 3:** Apply the alternating signs $(-1)^{i+j}$ to form the cofactor matrix.\n\n$$\nC=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 3 & 9 \\\\ 8 & -11 & -34 \\\\ -5 & 7 & 21\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n**Step 4:** Transpose the matrix of cofactors.\n\n$$\nC^T=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 8 & -5 \\\\ 3 & -11 & 7 \\\\ 9 & -34 & 21\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n**Step 5:** Divide by the nonzero determinant.\n\n$$\nA^{-1}=\\frac{1}{\\det(A)}C^T=1\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 8 & -5 \\\\ 3 & -11 & 7 \\\\ 9 & -34 & 21\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 8 & -5 \\\\ 3 & -11 & 7 \\\\ 9 & -34 & 21\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" 13194139544945 17592186576411 true] [17592186060458 1074 "Let's follow the standard five-step procedure.\n\n**Step 1:** Find the determinant of $A$.\n\n$$\n\\det(A)=(1)(5)+(-1)(-6)+(3)(-2)=5.\n$$\n\n**Step 2:** Form the matrix of minors. Delete row $i$ and column $j$ to obtain $M_{ij}$. For example,\n\n$$\nM_{11}=\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{vmatrix}=5.\n$$\n\nComputing the remaining minors gives\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 6 & -2 \\\\ 5 & 7 & -4 \\\\ -5 & -4 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n**Step 3:** Apply the alternating signs $(-1)^{i+j}$ to form the cofactor matrix.\n\n$$\nC=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & -6 & -2 \\\\ -5 & 7 & 4 \\\\ -5 & 4 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n**Step 4:** Transpose the matrix of cofactors.\n\n$$\nC^T=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & -5 & -5 \\\\ -6 & 7 & 4 \\\\ -2 & 4 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n**Step 5:** Divide by the nonzero determinant.\n\n$$\nA^{-1}=\\frac{1}{\\det(A)}C^T=\\frac{1}{5}\\begin{bmatrix}5 & -5 & -5 \\\\ -6 & 7 & 4 \\\\ -2 & 4 & 3\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & -1 \\\\ - \\frac{6}{5} & \\frac{7}{5} & \\frac{4}{5} \\\\ - \\frac{2}{5} & \\frac{4}{5} & \\frac{3}{5}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" 13194139544945 17592186576411 true] [17592186060459 1074 "Since $A^{-1}=\\dfrac{1}{\\det(A)}C^T,$ the entry $a_{23}^{\\prime}$ uses the cofactor $C_{32}$. Remove row $3$ and column $2$ to get\n\n$$\nC_{32}=(-1)^{3+2}\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{vmatrix}=-4.\n$$\n\nUsing the given determinant, we obtain\n\n$$\na_{23}^{\\prime}=\\frac{-4}{4-4k}.\n$$\n\nThe inverse exists only when $k\\ne1$. Set this entry equal to $-1$ and solve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{-4}{4-4k}&=-1\\\\-4&=-4+4k\\\\k&=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt $k=0,$ the determinant is $4\\ne0,$ so this value is valid." 13194139544945 17592186576411 true] [17592186084674 1078 #edb/ref 17592186084676 13194139544945 17592186576411 true] [17592186084686 1078 #edb/ref 17592186084690 13194139544945 17592186576411 true] [17592186086867 1078 #edb/ref 17592186086869 13194139544945 17592186576411 true] [17592186087990 1074 "The matrix $A$ is singular if and only if its determinant is $0$. Calculating the determinant, we get\n\n$$\n\\det(A)=k\\cdot k-2\\cdot2=k^2-4.\n$$\n\nNow, set the determinant equal to $0$ and solve for $k$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k^2-4&=0\\\\(k-2)(k+2)&=0\\\\k&=2\\quad\\text{or}\\quad k=-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $k=\\pm2$." 13194139544945 17592186576411 true] [17592186087992 1074 "Given a $2\\times2$ matrix, use the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}a&b\\\\c&d\\end{bmatrix}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{ad-bc}\\begin{bmatrix}d&-b\\\\-c&a\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFirst, compute the determinant:\n\n$$\n\\det(A)=(7)(1)-(2)(4)=-1.\n$$\n\nSince the determinant is nonzero, the inverse exists. Thus,\n\n$$\nA^{-1}=\\frac{1}{-1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ -4 & 7\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 2 \\\\ 4 & -7\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nMultiplying in either order gives the identity matrix, which verifies the result." 13194139544945 17592186576411 true] [17592186088369 1074 "Given a $2\\times2$ matrix, use the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}a&b\\\\c&d\\end{bmatrix}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{ad-bc}\\begin{bmatrix}d&-b\\\\-c&a\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFirst, compute the determinant:\n\n$$\n\\det(Z)=(9)(1)-(-1)(2)=11.\n$$\n\nSince the determinant is nonzero, the inverse exists. Thus,\n\n$$\nZ^{-1}=\\frac{1}{11}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 9\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{11} & \\frac{1}{11} \\\\ - \\frac{2}{11} & \\frac{9}{11}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nMultiplying in either order gives the identity matrix, which verifies the result." 13194139544945 17592186576411 true] [17592186111961 1078 #edb/ref 17592186111962 13194139544945 17592186576411 true] [17592186111967 1078 #edb/ref 17592186111969 13194139544945 17592186576411 true] [17592186113358 1078 #edb/ref 17592186113362 13194139544945 17592186576411 true] [17592186113406 1078 #edb/ref 17592186113409 13194139544945 17592186576411 true] [17592186114799 1078 #edb/ref 17592186114801 13194139544945 17592186576411 true] [17592186115492 1074 "First, write the system with the variables ordered $x,y$ on the left and the constants on the right. Insert a zero coefficient for any missing variable.\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}3 x + 5 y &=4 \\\\ x - 7 y &=-4\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNow, we can write the system in the form $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b},$ where\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 5 \\\\ 1 & -7\\end{bmatrix},\\qquad\\mathbf{x}=\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix},\\qquad\\mathbf{b}=\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nEach row of $A$ contains the coefficients from the corresponding equation. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 5 \\\\ 1 & -7\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" 13194139544945 17592186576411 true] [17592186115493 1074 "First, write the system with the variables ordered $x,y$ on the left and the constants on the right. Insert a zero coefficient for any missing variable.\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2 y &=3 \\\\ x &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNow, we can write the system in the form $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b},$ where\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\qquad\\mathbf{x}=\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix},\\qquad\\mathbf{b}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nEach row of $A$ contains the coefficients from the corresponding equation. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" 13194139544945 17592186576411 true] [17592186115732 1074 "First, write the system with the variables ordered $x,y$ on the left and the constants on the right. Insert a zero coefficient for any missing variable.\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}3 x + y &=10 \\\\ y &=-4\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNow, we can write the system in the form $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b},$ where\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix},\\qquad\\mathbf{x}=\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix},\\qquad\\mathbf{b}=\\begin{bmatrix}10 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nEach row of $A$ contains the coefficients from the corresponding equation. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}10 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" 13194139544945 17592186576411 true] [17592186115740 1074 "Multiplying the coefficient matrix by the column vector of variables gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}9 & 6 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}9 x + 6 y \\\\ x\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nEquating the corresponding entries, we get\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}9x+6y &=-3\\\\x &=-2\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nDividing the first equation by $3$ gives the equivalent system\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}3x+2y &=-1\\\\x &=-2.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$" 13194139544945 17592186576411 true] [17592186115977 1074 "First, write the system with the variables ordered $x,y$ on the left and the constants on the right. Insert a zero coefficient for any missing variable.\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}- 7 x + 2 y &=3 \\\\ - x + 2 y &=-1\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNow, we can write the system in the form $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b},$ where\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}-7 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix},\\qquad\\mathbf{x}=\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix},\\qquad\\mathbf{b}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nEach row of $A$ contains the coefficients from the corresponding equation. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-7 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" 13194139544945 17592186576411 true] [17592186119144 1078 #edb/ref 17592186119149 13194139544945 17592186576411 true] [17592186120505 1074 "First, to get the minor of $d_{33}$, remove the $3$rd row and $3$rd column:\n\n$$\nM_{33}=\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 5 & -6 & 8 \\\\ 4 & 5 & -7\\end{vmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThe cofactor includes the alternating sign $(-1)^{i+j}$. Therefore,\n\n$$\nC_{33}=(-1)^{3+3}M_{33}=M_{33}=\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 5 & -6 & 8 \\\\ 4 & 5 & -7\\end{vmatrix}.\n$$" 13194139544945 17592186576411 true] [17592186297543 1071 "Calculate ${A}^{-1},$ where\n$$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}-6 & 3 \\\\ -5 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n<div class=\"matrix-answer\">\n<span class=\"matrix-answer-label\">${A}^{-1}=\\hspace{0.222em}\\hspace{0.222em}$</span>\n<div class=\"matrix-an